          ],
          "confidence": 0.95,
          "synopsis": "Explains how to identify the least squares line from summary statistics: estimate the slope b1 = (sy/sx)R, use the point (x̄, ȳ) with the point-slope equation, and simplify to obtain b0 = ȳ − b1x̄. Example 8.10 applies this to Elmhurst aid and family income data, producing aid = 24,327 − 0.0431 × family income. A software summary table gives matching estimates for b0 and b1.",
          "pages": [
            359
          ],
          "figure_ids": [
            "fig_f213361bf057b0_5187_v2"
          ],
          "chunk_ids": [
            "chk_f213361bf057b0_945_v2",
            "chk_f213361bf057b0_946_v2"
          ],
          "hit_chunk_id": "chk_f213361bf057b0_945_v2",
          "hit_text": "To identify the least squares line from summary statistics:\n\n• Estimate the slope parameter, b1 = (sy/sx)R.\n• Noting that the point (¯x,y¯) is on the least squares line, use x0 = x¯ and y0 = y¯ with the point-slope equation: y − y¯ = b1(x − x¯).\n• Simplify the equation, which would reveal that b0 = y¯ − b1x¯.\n\nEXAMPLE 8.10 START Example problem: Using the point (101780,19940) from the sample means and the slope estimate\n\nb1 = −0.0431 from Guided Practice 8.9, ﬁnd the least-squares line for predicting aid based on family income.\n\nSolution to the example: Apply the point-slope equation using (101.78,19.94) and the slope b1 = −0.0431:\n\ny − y0 = b1(x − x0) y − 19,940 = −0.0431(x − 101,780)\n\nExpanding the right side and then adding 19,940 to each side, the equation simpliﬁes: aid = 24,327 − 0.0431 × family income\n\nHere we have replaced y with aid and x with family income to put the equation in context. The ﬁnal equation should always include a “hat” on the variable being predicted, whether it is a generic “y” or a named variable like “aid”.\n\nEXAMPLE 8.10 HAS ENDED.\n\nA computer is usually used to compute the least squares line, and a summary table generated using software for the Elmhurst regression line is shown in Figure 8.14. The ﬁrst column of numbers provides estimates for b0 and b1, respectively. These results match those from Example 8.10 (with some minor rounding error).\n\nEstimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|) (Intercept) 24319.3 1291.5 18.83 <0.0001 family income -0.0431 0.0108 -3.98 0.0002",
