          "confidence": 0.9,
          "synopsis": "Explains writing the least squares regression line for predicting gift aid from family income. Defines the intercept a and slope b as statistics computed from data. Gives formulas: slope b = r sy/sx, and intercept a = y¯ − bx¯ using the point of averages. Notes a computer is usually used, and presents a summary table of estimates for the Elmhurst College data.",
          "pages": [
            459
          ],
          "figure_ids": [
            "fig_0689cddaba206d_6295_v2"
          ],
          "chunk_ids": [
            "chk_0689cddaba206d_1169_v2"
          ],
          "hit_chunk_id": "chk_0689cddaba206d_1169_v2",
          "hit_text": "For the Elmhurst College data, we could ﬁt a least squares regression line for predicting gift aid based on a student’s family income and write the equation as:\n\naid̂ = a + b × family income\n\nHere a is the y-intercept of the least squares regression line and b is the slope of the least squares regression line. a and b are both statistics that can be calculated from the data.\n\nWe can enter all of the data into a statistical software package and easily ﬁnd the values of a and b. However, we can also calculate these values by hand, using only the summary statistics.\n\n• The slope of the least squares line is given by b = r sy sx where r is the correlation coeﬃcient between the variables x and y, and sx and sy are the sample standard deviations of x, the explanatory variable, and y, the response variable.\n• The point of averages (x,¯ y¯) is always on the least squares line. Plugging this point in for x and y in the least squares equation and solving for a gives\n\ny¯ = a + bx¯ a = y¯− bx¯\n\nWe mentioned earlier that a computer is usually used to compute the least squares line. A summary table based on computer output is shown in Figure 5.20 for the Elmhurst College data. The ﬁrst column of numbers provides estimates for a and b, respectively.\n\nEstimate Std. Error t value Pr(>∣t∣) (Intercept) 24.3193 1.2915 18.83 0.0000 family income -0.0431 0.0108 -3.98 0.0002\n\nFigure 5.20: Summary of least squares ﬁt for the Elmhurst College data.",
