          "confidence": 0.95,
          "synopsis": "Summarizes least-squares regression: the line minimizes squared residuals and passes through (x̄, ȳ); form ŷ = a + bx; R² is the proportion of variation in the response explained by the linear relationship, ranges 0 to 1, and does not indicate direction; a and b are sample statistics; slope b is the predicted change in y per one-unit increase in x; intercept a is the predicted y at x = 0, sometimes unreasonable due to extrapolation or impossible values; interpret a, b, and R² in context.",
          "pages": [
            473
          ],
          "figure_ids": [],
          "chunk_ids": [
            "chk_0689cddaba206d_1203_v2",
            "chk_0689cddaba206d_1204_v2"
          ],
          "hit_chunk_id": "chk_0689cddaba206d_1203_v2",
          "hit_text": "• The simple linear regression model is ﬁt to the data by minimizing the sum of the squares of the residuals. Because of this, the resulting equation is often called the least-squares regression line (LSRL) and is calculated using technology. This regression line will pass through the point (¯x , y¯).\n• We write the least squares regression line in the form: yˆ = a+bx. The slope b and the y-intercept a can be calculated using technology or identiﬁed from computer output.\n• In simple linear regression, the square of the correlation coeﬃcient, R2, is called the coeﬃcient of determination. R2 is the proportion of variation in the response variable that is explained by the linear relationship with the explanatory variable.\n• R2 is always between 0 and 1, inclusive, or between 0% and 100%, inclusive. R2 applies to any type of model, not just a linear model, and can be used to compare the ﬁt among various models. The higher the value of R2, the better the model “ﬁts” the data.\n• The value of R2 is always positive and cannot tell us the direction of the association. If ﬁnding r based on R2, make sure to use either the scatterplot or the slope of the regression line to determine whether r =+ √R2 or − √R2.\n• The coeﬃcients a and b of the least-squares regression line model (line of best ﬁt) are statistics because they are based on a sample of values.\n• The slope b of the least-squares regression line can be interpreted as the predicted increase or decrease in the response variable y for a one-unit increase in the explanatory variable x.",
