          ],
          "confidence": 0.85,
          "synopsis": "Guided Practice 8.8 asks whether least squares regression can be applied to the Elmhurst data; the solution notes a linear trend, no obvious outliers, roughly constant variance, and non-time-series observations. Section 8.2.4 sets up the least squares line aid = β0 + β1 × family income, defines β0 and β1 as parameters, and gives two properties for estimating them: b1 = (sy/sx)R and that (x̄, ȳ) lies on the line. Summary statistics for family income and gift aid are listed with R = −0.499.",
          "pages": [
            357,
            358
          ],
          "figure_ids": [],
          "chunk_ids": [
            "chk_f213361bf057b0_942_v2",
            "chk_f213361bf057b0_943_v2"
          ],
          "hit_chunk_id": "chk_f213361bf057b0_942_v2",
          "hit_text": "Should we have concerns about applying least squares regression to the Elmhurst data in Figure 8.11?10 Go to the preceding footnote link for the Guided Practice solution.\n\nGUIDED PRACTICE 8.8 HAS ENDED.\n\n10The trend appears to be linear, the data fall around the line with no obvious outliers, the variance is roughly constant. These are also not time series observations. Least squares regression can be applied to these data.\n\n8.2.4 Finding the least squares line For the Elmhurst data, we could write the equation of the least squares regression line as\n\naid = β0 + β1 × family income\n\nHere the equation is set up to predict gift aid based on a student’s family income, which would be useful to students considering Elmhurst. These two values, β0 and β1, are the parameters of the regression line.\n\nAs in Chapters 5, 6, and 7, the parameters are estimated using observed data. In practice, this estimation is done using a computer in the same way that other estimates, like a sample mean, can be estimated using a computer or calculator. However, we can also ﬁnd the parameter estimates by applying two properties of the least squares line:\n\n• The slope of the least squares line can be estimated by b1 = sy sx R where R is the correlation between the two variables, and sx and sy are the sample standard deviations of the explanatory variable and response, respectively.\n• If x¯ is the sample mean of the explanatory variable and y¯ is the sample mean of the vertical variable, then the point (¯x,y¯) is on the least squares line.",
